ogle Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google cite40†Google Research Explore our research†research.google L100: \nL101: Products and apps\nL102: cite35†Gemini app Chat with Gemini†gemini.google.com cite34†Google AI Studio Build with our next-gen AI models†aistudio.google.com cite41†Google Antigravity Our agentic development platform†antigravity.google L103: \nL104: cite0†Models L105: \nL106: cite10†Research L107: \nL108: cite17†Science L109: \nL110: cite26†About L111: \nL112: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L113: \nL114: February 12, 2026\nL115: # Decoding Safety Feedback from Diverse Raters: A Data-driven Lens on Responsiveness to Severity\nL116: \nL117: cite42†View publication†openreview.net L118: ## Abstract\nL119: Ensuring the safety of Generative AI requires a nuanced understanding of pluralistic viewpoints. In this paper, we introduce a novel data-driven approach for analyzing ordinal safety ratings in pluralistic settings. Specifically, we address the challenge of interpreting nuanced differences in safety feedback from a diverse population expressed via ordinal scales (e.g., a Likert scale).\nL120: We define non-parametric responsiveness metrics that quantify how raters convey broader distinctions and granular variations in the severity of safety violations. Leveraging publicly available datasets of pluralistic safety feedback as our case studies, we investigate how raters from different demographic groups use an ordinal scale to express their perceptions of the severity of violations.\nL121: We apply our metrics across violation types, demonstrating their utility in extracting nuanced insights that are crucial for aligning AI systems reliably in multi-cultural contexts. We show that our approach can inform rater selection and feedback interpretation by capturing nuanced viewpoints across different demographic groups, hence improving the quality of pluralistic data collection and in turn contributing to more robust AI alignment.\nL122: ## Authors\nL123: \nL124: Pushkar Mishra, Lora Aroyo, Verena Rieser, Charvi Rastogi, Stephen Pfohl, Roma Patel, Ding Wang, Alicia Parrish, Mark Díaz, Vinodkumar Prabhakaran, Tian Huey Teh\nL125: \nL126: ## Venue\nL127: \nL128: TMLR (Transactions on ML)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn31666view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.zhipuai.cn/zh/research\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.zhipuai.cn/zh/research: (400) Timeout fetching\n"
